Because the account was created September 1, 2026—after the August 31 cutoff—Fable 5.1 enforces conversation-bound thinking blocks.

Your client must do one of these:

- Preferably keep history append-only: do not rewrite `system`, `tools`, or earlier messages. Add changed instructions as a new in-conversation system message.
- When compacting client-side, start a fresh history containing the summary and new user turn. Do not replay old `thinking` or `redacted_thinking` blocks.
- If retaining recent turns after a summary, strip their thinking blocks while preserving `text` and `tool_use`.
- Do not compact during an unfinished `tool_use`/`tool_result` round.

Otherwise, replayed thinking will fail with a 400 because it is cryptographically bound to the original conversation prefix.

`drop_block` is not a quality optimization. It is a compatibility/recovery mechanism: the API discards the first mismatched thinking block and all later thinking blocks, so the request succeeds but the model loses that prior reasoning. It may therefore reduce continuity or quality. Use it deliberately, log `input_transformations`, and include the `thinking-binding-controls-2026-08-01` beta header:

```json
{
  "thinking": {
    "type": "adaptive",
    "block_binding": {
      "prefix_mismatch_behavior": "drop_block"
    }
  }
}
```

The default is `"error"`, which is preferable in testing because it exposes unintended history mutation. [`drop_block` documentation](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/errors) and [Fable 5.1 prompting guidance](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/prompting-claude-fable-5-1).

