No. A shared preset is invalid because the requested settings are contradictory and the providers expose different controls.

| Model | Thinking disabled | `reasoning_effort: high` | Preserved thinking |
|---|---|---|---|
| `kimi-k3` | Unsupported; always thinks | Supported | Always required; replay the complete assistant message |
| `kimi-k2.7-code` | Unsupported; always thinks | Don’t send unless the endpoint explicitly documents it | Always enabled |
| `kimi-k2.6` | Supported | Don’t send unless documented | `thinking.keep: "all"`—but useful only with thinking enabled |
| `glm-5.3` | Unsupported; forced thinking | Supported | `clear_thinking: false` |

Use model-specific adapters, even if they inherit a common intent such as “high effort with preserved reasoning”:

```jsonc
// kimi-k3
{
  "reasoning_effort": "high"
  // no thinking.type; preserve and replay full assistant messages
}
```

```jsonc
// kimi-k2.7-code
{
  "thinking": { "type": "enabled", "keep": "all" }
}
```

```jsonc
// kimi-k2.6
{
  "thinking": { "type": "enabled", "keep": "all" }
}
```

```jsonc
// glm-5.3
{
  "thinking": {
    "type": "enabled",
    "clear_thinking": false
  },
  "reasoning_effort": "high"
}
```

For GLM, explicitly set `clear_thinking: false` on both routes: Coding Plan enables preservation by default, while the standard API disables it by default. In both cases, replay every historical `reasoning_content` block completely, unchanged, and in order.

If “thinking disabled” is genuinely mandatory, none of `kimi-k3`, `kimi-k2.7-code`, or `glm-5.3` qualifies; only `kimi-k2.6` can honor it, and preserved thinking plus high reasoning effort then has no meaningful effect. [Kimi K3 usage](https://github.com/MoonshotAI/Kimi-K3/blob/main/README.md), [GLM thinking behavior](https://docs.z.ai/guides/capabilities/thinking-mode), [GLM API parameters](https://docs.z.ai/api-reference/llm/chat-completion).

No. Those settings conflict and are not portable as one shared preset.

- `thinking: disabled` means no new reasoning is generated, so `reasoning_effort: high` is ineffective or may be rejected.
- Preserved Thinking only helps when thinking is enabled and prior `reasoning_content` is replayed unchanged.
- Kimi K3, K2.7 Code, and K2.6 differ in supported effort/off behavior; some Kimi variants effectively require thinking.
- For GLM, use `thinking.type: "enabled"` with `reasoning_effort: "high"`. On the standard API, add `thinking.clear_thinking: false`; Coding Plan enables preservation by default. The two GLM routes also require different base URLs. [Z.AI Thinking Mode](https://docs.z.ai/guides/capabilities/thinking-mode), [Coding Plan endpoint](https://docs.z.ai/devpack/quick-start)

Use a shared conceptual preset—“high effort + preserve thinking”—but render it per provider/endpoint:

```json
// Kimi
{
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "thinking": {
    "type": "enabled",
    "keep": "all"
  }
}
```

```json
// GLM standard API
{
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "thinking": {
    "type": "enabled",
    "clear_thinking": false
  }
}
```

```json
// GLM Coding Plan
{
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "thinking": {
    "type": "enabled"
  }
}
```

If “thinking disabled” is an absolute requirement, make that a separate preset and omit both high effort and preservation.
